  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach relies heavily on gradient-based methods with smoothing, which often gets trapped in local optima. The new strategy adopts a multi-resolution framework with hybrid optimization techniques: combining derivative-free global search with derivative-based local refinement. The primary bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration capability of gradient-based methods. By employing several smooth cycle augmentations that transfer information across resolutions and integrate gradient-free exploration, we systematically search across function spaces of varying complexities. The core difference lies in: (1) using mixed-integer representation for candidate functions, (2) applying smooth cycle augmentation via upward/downward scaling to escape local minima, and (3) incorporating sparse gradient projections for efficient computation. This addresses the mathematical challenges of non-convex optimization by leveraging dual representations and resolution independence.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: h(x) as a piecewise constant function on [0,2] with discontinuities handled through regularized projections\n- Variables: h[i] \u2208 [0,1]^n_points, n_points \u2265 200\n- Objective: min C5 = (2/n) * max\u2096 |\u222b_{-\u221e}^{\u221e} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx|  \n  Computed via: max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * (2.0/n)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- Formulation:  \n  minimize: max_k [\u222b_0^2 h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx]  \n  subject to: h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1  \n  The optimization is nonconvex due to products within the integral.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize h0:\n  - If initial_h_values provided: use provided array (n=200)\n  - Else: Generate normal random samples, project to feasible set\nPhase 1 (10% budget): Multi-resolution parent activation\n  1. Sample base functions:  \n      a. Standard two-peak structure  \n      b. Uniform sparse distribution  \n      c. Broken-line interpolation of parent patterns  \n  2. Projection: Apply constrained least-squares projection to satisfy integral\n  3. Objective caching: Compute initial C5 before optimization\nPhase 2 (60% budget): Smooth cycle enhancement\n  1. For each new resolution:  \n      a. Scale vectors: strict doubling (n\u21922n) or geometric progression (n\u00d71.5)  \n      b. Augmentation: Add cyclic perturbations preserving support structure  \n      c. Cycle projection: Maintaining box constraints and integral normalization  \n  2. Perform gradient steps:  \n      \u2022 Initialize with projected normal step  \n      \u2022 Compute cyclic correlation gradient w.r.t. the scaled basis\nPhase 3 (30% budget): Direct search refinement\n  1. Implement sparse-gradient descent:  \n      \u2022 Iterate until improvement threshold  \n      \u2022 Maintain Cauchy-like exploration  \n  2. For fine-tuning:  \n      Structure-preserving mutations  \n      Local max ascent  \nPhase 4 (Fallback): Feasibility repair\n  1. If unfeasible candidate: Find nearest point satisfying  \n     (a) Strict bounds [0,1] with \u03b5-perturbation  \n     (b) Integral approximation within 1% tolerance  \n  2. Use iterative scaling method for bound adjustment\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nBase configuration:\n  - Resolution progression: 100 \u2192 200 factor by 2  \n  - Cycle smoothing parameter \u03b1: 80-300 range (warmed up)  \n  - Saddle escape by:  \n      \u2022 Strict projection (if plateaued)  \n      \u2022 Instruction-adaptive perturbation  \nOptimization tree variations:\n  1. Simulated annealing: Temperature schedule 300\u2192100\u00b7exp(-iteration/1000)\n  2. Dimension exclusion: Disabling dimension 50% during optimization\n  3. Multi-start refinement: Launch 3-5 independent restarts per resolution\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\nStaged allocation:\n  Time check points at:  \n    10%, 40%, 80% total time  \n  Memory profiling on:  \n    - h-vector storage (n_points integers)  \n    - Profiler graphs (windowed storage for top 2% improvements)  \nFallback protocol:  \n  - Return closest candidate if >\u221a2*n_points improvement unseen  \n  - Repair norm preservation: L1/L2 restoration\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:  \n  (a) Bounds: np.all([0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1])  \n  (b) Integral: np.sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 within machine_eps  \n  (c) Finite C5 value  \nObjective consistency:  \n  Compare computed C5(v) against theoretical bounds  \n  Ensure projection maintains internal structure  \nReturn structure:  \n  (h, C5_value, n_points)  \n  Unless failure, ensure properties:  \n    np.min(h) >= -1e-6, np.max(h) <= 1e-6, np.sum(h) == n_points/2 (within float tolerance)",
